    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_edge_by_id - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_edge_by_id.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_edge_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add an edge between two nodes using their integer IDs.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_edge_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add an edge between two nodes using their integer IDs.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_edge_by_id()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add an edge between two nodes using their integer IDs.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd an edge between two nodes using their integer IDs.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L2206-L2360){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_edge_by_id(\n    source,\n    target,\n    rel=None,\n    weight=None,\n    **attributes,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Add edge by ID](../assets/graphs/add-edge-by-id.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Before: four nodes tagged with their integer ids, A 1, B 2, C 3, D 4, joined by three edges; the ids 3 and 4 are highlighted. After: a new accent edge joins node 3 (C) and node 4 (D), closing the cycle; no nodes are added.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA strict version of `add_edge()` that only accepts integer node IDs — no label\nresolution. Both source and target must be valid node IDs that already exist in the\ngraph.\n\nBecause nothing is looked up by label, this variant avoids any ambiguity when labels are\nduplicated or look like numbers (a node labelled `\"7\"` is never confused with node ID\n`7`), and it fails loudly if a label is passed by mistake. It's a good fit for code that\nworks with IDs obtained from other methods, such as `get_node_ids()`, selections, or the\n`id` column of the node DataFrame.\n\nAs with `add_edge()`, each call creates a new edge with its own edge ID (so repeated pairs\nproduce parallel edges), self-loops are allowed, and the graph is modified in place. To add\nmany ID-based edges at once, use `add_edges_by_id()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The source node ID. Must be a Python `int` (strings and NumPy integer types are\n    rejected) referring to an existing node. In a directed graph the edge starts here.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The target node ID, with the same requirements as `source=`. In a directed graph the\n    edge points to this node.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional relationship type for the edge (e.g., `\"follows\"`). Stored in the first-class\n    `rel` column of the edge DataFrame. Default is `None`.\n\n<code>[weight]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional numeric edge weight. The value is stored on the edge whether or not the graph\n    was created with `weighted=True`. Default is `None` (no weight).\n\n<code>[**attributes]{.doc-parameter-name}</code>\n\n:   Arbitrary key-value pairs to store as edge attributes. Each key becomes a column in the\n    edge DataFrame.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Returns self to enable method chaining.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [TypeError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or target is not an integer.\n\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or target node ID does not exist in the graph.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_edge`) \n\n:   Add an edge by label or ID.\n\n[](`~add_edges_by_id`) \n\n:   Add many edges between node IDs.\n\n[](`~delete_edge_by_id`) \n\n:   Remove an edge by its ID.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nLook up the IDs first, then connect the nodes by ID. The new edge appears in the edge\nDataFrame with the given `rel`:\n\n::: {#40613c7a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_nodes([\"Alice\", \"Bob\"])\n\nalice_id = graph.get_id_from_label(\"Alice\")\nbob_id = graph.get_id_from_label(\"Bob\")\ngraph.add_edge_by_id(source=alice_id, target=bob_id, rel=\"follows\")\n\ngraph.get_edge_df()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (1, 4)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>&quot;follows&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nPassing a label instead of an ID raises a `TypeError` rather than silently resolving it:\n\n::: {#784edb59 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntry:\n    graph.add_edge_by_id(\"Alice\", \"Bob\")\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nsource must be an integer node ID, got str. Use add_edge() to reference nodes by label.\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Refunds from the Grocer\n\nIDs usually come from another method. In `payments()`, `get_predecessors()` gives the IDs\nof the eight accounts that paid the Grocer, and each of them gets a refund by ID, with no\nlabels looked up:\n\n::: {#bcaf61c4 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments = ec.payments()\ngrocer = payments.get_id_from_label(\"Grocer\")\n\ncustomers = payments.get_predecessors(grocer)\nfor customer in customers:\n    payments.add_edge_by_id(grocer, customer, weight=5, refund=True)\n\nlen(customers), payments.get_out_degree(\"Grocer\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(8, 8)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe refunds are the only edges with a `refund` attribute, so `highlight_edges=\"refund\"`\npicks them out:\n\n::: {#480d115c .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments.plot(highlight_edges=\"refund\", label_by=\"label\", seed=3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,4